  "Number": 23,
  "Question": "If \\(X,\\) \\(Y,\\) and \\(Z\\) are different digits, then the largest possible \\(3\\)-digit sum for\n\n\\[\\begin{array}{cr} & XXX \\\\ & YX \\\\ + & X \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}\\]\n\nhas the form",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(XXY\\)",
    "B": "\\(XYZ\\)",
    "C": "\\(YYX\\)",
    "D": "\\(YYZ\\)",
    "E": "\\(ZZY\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The requirement that the sum have three digits rules out \\(X=9\\). Even the first and last addends would then give\n\\[\n999+9=1008,\n\\]\nwhich already has four digits. Thus \\(X\\leq8\\).\n\nIt follows that \\(XXX\\) is at most 888, \\(YX\\) is at most 98, and the last addend is at most 8. Therefore the entire sum is at most\n\\[\n888+98+8=994.\n\\]\nThese three upper bounds are attained together by choosing \\(X=8\\) and \\(Y=9\\). The result is 994, so we can take \\(Z=4\\), which is different from both 8 and 9.\n\nThus the largest three-digit sum is \\(994=YYZ\\), choice \\(\\text{D}\\).",
